{"success": true, "data": {"stdout": "\begin{abstract}
Modular LLM agents that route between neural reasoning and deterministic computation promise improved reliability, yet understanding \emph{what} drives the value of routing decisions remains unclear. We propose episode-level counterfactual analysis: running complete episodes under pure strategies and comparing final outcomes to decompose routing signal into death-avoidance and non-death components. Among task types exhibiting meaningful routing signal in prescreening (11 of 30 available ScienceWorld task types, 245 episodes, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct), 78.9\% of non-tie routing labels (75 of 95) are driven by death asymmetry---a penalty-independent metric. Under the default penalty $P{=}100$, the Death Avoidance Factor (DAF) is $90.4\%$ (95\% CI: $[81.6\%, 98.8\%]$), though DAF varies from 50\% ($P{=}10$) to 98\% ($P{=}500$) across penalty settings (Appendix~\ref{app:penalty}). Per-task death rate correlates strongly with routing signal ($r{=}0.97$, though partly structural as death penalties contribute directly to score differences; \S\ref{sec:death-decomp}). An exploratory step-level router trained on these labels achieves $54.1 \pm 5.4\%$ step-level balanced accuracy (with same-episode leakage) but only $47.1 \pm 2.2\%$ on held-out episodes (below the always-deterministic baseline of 50\%); data scaling ($32{\times}$) confirms this capacity-dependent limitation; scaling to 4B yields $59.0\%$ episode-level accuracy ($p < 0.005$ vs.\ random) but the cost (4B parameters for $+9$\,pp) is disproportionate to the routing benefit. Cross-environment analysis on MATH ($N{=}300$) shows the death-driven routing signal is not statistically detectable without a death penalty: the label distribution inverts from 86:9 (deterministic-preferred) to 30:17 (internal directionally favored), with a directional trend favoring internal reasoning ($+4.3$ percentage points, $p = 0.079$, not reaching $\alpha{=}0.05$). A Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct replication (7 task types, 70 episodes) shows direction-consistent death patterns for all task types, with a larger death-rate differential ($+34.3\%$ vs.\ $+26.6\%$ for Qwen) and zero internal-preferred episodes. These findings indicate that in environments with catastrophic failure modes, routing value is dominated by safety---the routing problem reduces to safety-first modality selection rather than efficiency optimization.
\end{abstract}
