\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Cross-environment evidence.}
Does routing signal persist without catastrophic failure? On MATH Level~4--5 (300 problems), using a code interpreter as the deterministic modality, internal reasoning directionally outperforms: 81.0\% vs.\ 76.7\% accuracy ($+4.3$~pp, 95\% CI $[0.0, +8.7]$; McNemar $p = 0.079$, not reaching $\alpha{=}0.05$). The label distribution inverts: 30 internal-preferred vs.\ 17 deterministic-preferred (cf.\ 9:86 in ScienceWorld), with 84.3\% no-preference. In the absence of death risk, the death-driven routing signal is not statistically detectable---consistent with death avoidance as the primary driver. A complementary evaluation on ALFWorld ($N{=}135$) confirms that the counterfactual framework identifies different routing mechanisms across environments: the deterministic advantage there stems from information asymmetry (access to admissible actions) rather than death avoidance (Appendix~\ref{app:cross-env}).

\paragraph{Penalty sensitivity.}
The $r = 0.97$ correlation is specific to catastrophic death (score $= -100$); defining ``failure'' as any non-positive score yields $r = -0.13$ ($p = 0.70$). The correlation emerges only at $P \geq 50$ ($r = 0.86$) and reflects that non-death episodes show near-zero performance differential ($\Delta_{\text{nd}} = 2.84$; Appendix~\ref{app:penalty}). Death is effectively the \emph{only} component generating differential routing signal in ScienceWorld. These findings may be capability-dependent: the 42.9\% internal death rate reflects a 7B model's hallucination vulnerability; larger models with lower death rates would reduce DAF, potentially revealing non-death routing signal currently masked by catastrophic failures.

\paragraph{Implications for router design.}
The dominance of death avoidance (DAF $= 90.4\%$ at $P{=}100$) reframes modality routing as primarily a \emph{safety} problem rather than an efficiency-quality tradeoff. The routing problem reduces to safety-first modality selection---closer to a conservative default policy than to a learned safety critic---aligning more closely with safe RL~\citep{garcia2015comprehensive} than with cost-quality optimization~\citep{cogrouter-2026, ares-2026}. The extreme label imbalance (86:9 deterministic-preferred, 150 no-preference) is not an artifact but a central result: among episodes where modality matters, deterministic is preferred at 9.6:1, explaining why always-deterministic is a strong baseline and why the trained router (\S\ref{sec:router-diagnostic}) adds no value. A lightweight death predictor may capture most routing benefit without full counterfactual labeling. The capacity scaling results reinforce this: extracting episode-level routing signal requires 4B parameters for only $+9$\,pp above random ($59.0\%$), a disproportionate cost that validates our counterfactual analytical methodology as the efficient alternative to brute-force learned routing. In principle, routing could be fully reduced to per-task-type death-risk prediction, bypassing step-level observation entirely---a direction we leave for future work.

\paragraph{Modality heterogeneity.}
The deliberate heterogeneity in deterministic modality definition---ground-truth state observation in ScienceWorld, code execution in MATH, admissible-action filtering in ALFWorld---tests whether the framework (episode-level counterfactual analysis) generalizes, not whether any single modality definition does. The consistent finding across structurally different modalities strengthens rather than weakens the conclusion that catastrophic-failure avoidance is the dominant routing signal wherever it exists.
