We introduced episode-level counterfactual analysis for decomposing routing signal in modular LLM agents. Applied to 245 ScienceWorld episodes (11 of 30 task types, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct), 78.9\% of non-tie routing labels (75 of 95) arise from death asymmetry. The DAF reaches $90.4\%$ (95\% CI: $[81.6\%, 98.8\%]$) at $P{=}100$, with strong cross-task correlation ($r = 0.97$, $p < 10^{-6}$).

Cross-environment evidence supports this finding: on MATH ($N{=}300$), the death-driven routing signal is not statistically detectable without death penalty ($+4.3$~pp, $p = 0.079$, not reaching $\alpha{=}0.05$). A Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct replication (7 task types, 70 episodes) confirms direction-consistent death patterns for all tasks with a larger differential ($+34.3\%$ vs.\ $+26.6\%$). An exploratory router trained on counterfactual labels achieves only $47.1 \pm 2.2\%$ on held-out episodes---below always-deterministic---a capacity-dependent limitation confirmed by data scaling; the 4B scale yields $59.0\%$ episode-level accuracy but the cost is disproportionate to the routing benefit.

In environments with catastrophic failure modes, routing value is dominated by safety: the routing problem reduces to safety-first modality selection rather than efficiency optimization. Open directions include step-level counterfactual labeling, stronger backbones with lower intrinsic death rates, and environments with graded failure costs.
